 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample, as it involves a fixed number of texture lookups and noise evaluations. The cost depends on the number of octaves in the noise function.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory, plus the storage for the 2D Weather Map, 3D Noise Textures, and Cloud LUT.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Infinite resolution, no storage limits for cloud extent, dynamic control over cloud types and weather patterns.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of noise parameters to avoid repetitive patterns. Performance can degrade if high-frequency noise requires many octaves.
-*   **When to use:** Real-time rendering of large-scale atmospheric clouds where memory bandwidth is a constraint for storing 3D textures.
-*   **When not to use:** When highly specific, non-repeating cloud formations are required that cannot be achieved through noise variation.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample, as it involves a fixed number of noise evaluations and arithmetic operations [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the storage of noise textures and Cloud Maps. 3D noise textures can be large, but procedural generation allows for on-the-fly evaluation if textures are not stored [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Real-time capable on PC/Console hardware when using optimized noise functions and tileable textures [S2, unofficial]. Frame rates can drop to ~20 FPS for high-resolution ($1024^3$) volumes if computed on CPU, but GPU evaluation is significantly faster [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Infinite resolution, coherent geometry, easy control via weather maps, and efficient streaming [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of noise parameters to avoid artifacts; erosion logic can be complex to balance [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Procedural volume cloud modeling has evolved from simple height-based gradients to complex noise-driven systems. Early methods relied on 3D textures storing density or distance fields [S2, unofficial]. Modern approaches, such as those in Unity HDRP, utilize Perlin-Worley noise for shape and Perlin noise for detail, combined with 2D weather maps for artistic control [S1, S2, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in PC and console games for real-time sky rendering [S2, unofficial].
+Originating from the need for realistic, real-time volumetric clouds in games and simulations, this method draws from early work on procedural noise [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unity HDRP 12.0 popularized the use of 3D Perlin-Worley noise for cloud modeling [S2, unofficial]. Physical models, such as those by [S4, unofficial], provide the thermodynamic basis for cumulus convection, though procedural methods often approximate these effects for performance.
 
